## Security-sensitive and dual-use capabilities

The planned system will:

- use a Telethon Session to control an authenticated Telegram user account;
- observe incoming messages and outgoing messages sent by the account owner;
- generate automatic and proactive messages under the same Telegram identity;
- retain canonical conversation revisions and derived long-term memories;
- send selected private-message revisions, captions, validated images, active memories, and summaries to administrator-configured Memory Agent endpoints for extraction or consolidation;
- send eligible memory, summary, and canonical text chunks to an administrator-configured Embedding endpoint;
- send rule-filtered private relationship, activity, event, intention, and accepted active-memory data to an administrator-configured Proactive Agent endpoint, then send the selected topic and bounded private text context to the Main AI endpoint for final proactive-message generation;
- allow an administrator to change conversation modes and non-secret model configuration through a Control Bot, while the Telegram Web App remains key-only;
- present COPILOT response or proactive drafts to an allowlisted administrator through the Control Bot for explicit send, edit, or ignore decisions;
- present active-memory summaries, memory candidates, minimal evidence excerpts, and forget confirmations to an allowlisted administrator through the Control Bot;
- show an allowlisted administrator metadata about the exact context assembled for a model run and, only after a separate short-lived confirmation, copy that context's canonical text into the administrator's Control Bot chat;
- store model endpoint credentials and use them for outbound model API requests;
- maintain persistent account, database, cache, queue, and model configuration state;
- expose only a Caddy-managed HTTPS TCP 443 route for the key-only Web App, with no public HTTP fallback, database, Redis, health, metrics, or general management route;
- create encrypted off-host PostgreSQL/WAL and Telethon Session backups, plus an independent content-free erasure-ledger snapshot; and
- make outbound connections to an S3-compatible backup target, an ACME certificate service, and an explicitly configured external alert target in addition to Telegram and model providers.

These capabilities can support a legitimate personal digital twin, but misuse or credential compromise could enable impersonation, privacy intrusion, unwanted messaging, or unauthorized account control.

## Intended use and authorization

The intended use is operation by the owner of the Telegram account for the owner's personal communications. Deployment and operation require authorization from the account owner and must comply with Telegram rules, model-provider policies, applicable privacy requirements, and the expectations of affected contacts.

The project is not intended for:

- stealing, importing, or using another person's Telegram Session or credentials;
- covert monitoring or collection of another person's private communications;
- spam, phishing, bulk unsolicited messaging, or automated contact harvesting;
- bypassing Telegram controls, account restrictions, or provider safeguards; or
- accessing systems, accounts, or data without authorization.

## Implemented and planned data and network behavior

The design currently specifies:

- PostgreSQL storage for canonical messages and editable history, versioned memories and summaries, configuration versions, decisions, embeddings, jobs, and audit state;
- Redis for queues, cache, short-lived locks, notifications, and service heartbeat state;
- a persistent Telethon Session file mounted only by the Telegram `app` during runtime, with a read-only one-shot backup/restore exception while `app` is stopped;
- a private media volume for validated incoming images, with no general public media route;
- a 2 vCPU, 4 GiB RAM, 40 GiB SSD resource profile with a 10 GiB media quota and disk-pressure gates that stop media/background work and eventually block automated sending;
- outbound connections to Telegram, administrator-configured model endpoints, S3-compatible backup storage, ACME, and an explicitly configured alert target;
- Control Bot messages that may contain a minimum contact label and COPILOT draft text in the administrator's Telegram chat;
- Memory Agent requests that may contain selected private-message text, captions, validated images, related active memories, and summaries, plus Embedding requests that contain eligible derived or canonical text chunks;
- text-only Proactive Agent requests that contain only deterministic candidate reasons, typed evidence, time/relationship/activity and budget summaries, and bounded accepted active memory; selected candidates may then cause text-only Main AI requests containing the chosen topic, relationship/time context, bounded active memory, and recent committed message text;
- Control Bot memory-review messages that may contain a minimum contact label, memory/candidate summary, confidence/source category, and minimal evidence excerpt in the administrator's Telegram chat;
- metadata-only `/context` responses that contain manifest identifiers, budgets, counts, reasons, freshness, and versions but no private-message, memory, summary, or trusted-prompt text;
- explicitly confirmed `/context_preview` messages that may copy private-message text, memories, summaries, and trusted prompt text into the administrator's Control Bot chat; images are represented only by media reference, hash, MIME type, dimensions, and detail metadata, not by retransmitted binary data;
- context-preview request, token, and delivery records that contain identifiers, hashes, Bot message IDs, deadlines, and deletion results but never the preview body; confirmation tokens expire after five minutes by default, and known Bot messages are scheduled for best-effort deletion after ten minutes by default;
- a public HTTPS route limited to the key-only Telegram Web App and its dedicated credential API; and
- no general public database, Redis, health, or management endpoint.

Encrypted data exports are not served by the Web App, Caddy, or Telegram Bot. The design writes them to root-only host staging using a maintainer-provided public encryption recipient; a host operator retrieves them through an existing restricted SSH/SFTP management channel, and the server deletes the artifact within 24 hours.

The key-only Web App design uses Telegram-signed `initData`, an allowlisted administrator, a role/action-bound one-time launch token, and five-minute default freshness. API keys are encrypted with AES-256-GCM and a versioned host-mounted keyring. The page must not store keys in browser storage or load third-party analytics, but JavaScript, keyboards, browsers, operating systems, and Telegram clients may still retain transient copies outside the server's control.

Canonical message and memory content is designed to remain queryable in PostgreSQL, protected by host-disk encryption, encrypted backups, network isolation, and database permissions. Full raw Telegram updates and provider request or response payloads are disabled by default; temporary raw diagnostic capture, when explicitly enabled, must be separately encrypted and have a mandatory expiration.

Canonical messages and active memories do not automatically expire by default. Validated image originals expire after 30 days and metadata-free provider copies after 24 hours; media is not included in remote backup. Active memory candidates expire after 30 days. Terminal COPILOT/review text is redacted within 30 minutes, proactive topic text after 30 days, model/outbound attempt metadata after 30 days, non-content audit metadata after 365 days, and export artifacts after 24 hours. Raw diagnostic capture is disabled by default and, if explicitly enabled, is separately encrypted and limited to one hour. Message edits retain version history, while a Telegram delete removes content from every stored revision and associated media, then isolates or redacts derived data before leaving non-content tombstone and audit metadata.

PostgreSQL backup is designed to use continuous WAL archiving, daily differential backups, weekly full backups, and approximately four weeks of encrypted off-host retention. The Telethon Session is designed to be backed up daily and before upgrades into a separate encrypted repository with seven-day retention. The credential master key, database backup credentials, Session backup credentials, diagnostic key, and erasure-ledger key are separated. Restoration must overlay the latest independent erasure ledger and remain in bootstrap maintenance until schema, credentials, Session authorization, and unknown Telegram sends are reconciled. The 15-minute database RPO and two-hour whole-host RTO are unverified targets until restore drills provide evidence.

The existing local command still only parses an allowlisted local configuration, rejects credential-like keys and every external-integration flag, emits allowlisted JSON status metadata, and exits. Default tests deny non-loopback network access; loopback is allowed for the Windows asyncio socket pair and the explicit Web App browser harness. Database/Redis modules, credential mutation components, the M3 Telegram gateway, and the M4 orchestrator run only when explicitly constructed with injected dependencies; public CI uses disposable passwordless synthetic services, generated test keys, synthetic Bot tokens, deterministic Telegram/provider fakes, and no Telegram/provider/publication credential. M2 builds provider wire requests only through injected transports. M3 production-shaped Telegram requests are contract-tested without a connected client. M4 can authorize fake AUTO/COPILOT intents only after persistent version, lease, source, and mode gates; its provider input is still an injected hash-only M0 port because full context assembly belongs to M5. No default command starts the Web App, accepts a real key, constructs the Telethon adapter, runs the conversation scheduler, or sends a Telegram request. The implementation performs no telemetry or backup and exposes no default live automatic-reply path. The V1 design sends no product analytics or vendor telemetry; operational metrics remain private, while configured alert destinations would receive only content-free status metadata.

## Implemented safeguards through M4

The M0 scaffold currently implements and tests:

- typed opaque identifiers, UTC/monotonic time, revisions, versions, explicit result/evidence states, stable hashing, and redacted-value wrappers;
- framework-neutral Clock, Random, ID, transaction, queue, Telegram, generation, and embedding ports, with deterministic fakes only;
- a configuration allowlist that rejects credential-like keys, placeholder instance identifiers, unknown project keys, and all external integration enablement;
- allowlist-only structured logging that rejects content/secret fields, wrapped sensitive values, and test sentinels without echoing rejected values;
- deterministic synthetic fixtures, virtual time/randomness, a replayable state-machine harness, strict pytest markers, and non-loopback network denial;
- hash-locked bootstrap/runtime/development/lock-generation dependency sets and a pinned official CPython CI image digest;
- strict format, lint, typing, import-boundary, coverage, Markdown-link, Disclosure, private-path, secret, build-content, and signed-commit-presence gates; and
- an acceptance-manifest schema and generator that bind content-free evidence to a clean signed commit, source tree, lock hashes, and the canonical Disclosure.

M1 additionally implements and tests:

- a digest-pinned PostgreSQL 17.10/pgvector 0.8.6 and Redis 8.2.8 compatibility set, with hash-locked SQLAlchemy, Alembic, psycopg, arq, redis-py, and Testcontainers clients;
- an explicit Alembic baseline for account/peer/conversation, message event/revision/media, mode/control/turn, durable job/outbox, audit, erasure, and migration-progress tables, using named constraints, composite account-scope foreign keys, UUIDv7 entity IDs, and monotonic event identities;
- explicit `READ COMMITTED` transaction boundaries, repository/UoW detachment, conversation version CAS, PostgreSQL advisory locks, `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` job claims, owner/fencing-token lease completion, and expired-owner recovery;
- a PostgreSQL transactional outbox and content-free Redis/arq notification format containing only job ID and dispatch generation, with duplicate notification IDs and PostgreSQL rebuild after Redis loss;
- a database CHECK plus trigger enforcing immutable message revisions except one-way content-free redaction, a normal query path that excludes tombstones/redacted revisions, resumable erasure progress, and a content-free HMAC ledger schema;
- separate migrator, app, control, worker, backup, and maintenance role grants, including runtime denial of audit update/delete; and
- synthetic unit/integration tests and machine-readable migration/acceptance manifests for migration round trips, constraints, CAS/lease/fencing, Redis loss, role denial, redaction, interrupted progress, and synthetic EXPLAIN eligibility.

These controls establish the M1 durable-state boundary. GitLab Linux pipeline #9 validated the exact signed commit against disposable PostgreSQL 17.10/pgvector 0.8.6 and Redis 8.2.8, including migration, role, constraint, recovery, redaction, and synthetic EXPLAIN paths.

M2 additionally implements and tests:

- four independent model profiles, immutable canonical config/capability/endpoint versions, draft/profile CAS, current-key resolution for new runs, and non-reused credential version numbers after deletion;
- canonical request/response normalization for Responses, Chat Completions, Messages, and Embedding, with explicit rejection of legacy text `/completions`, image detail fixed to `auto`, structured/stream/error fixtures, and injected transports only;
- AES-256-GCM credential envelopes with random 96-bit nonces, deployment/role/profile/credential/version AAD, versioned master keys, one-way ciphertext destruction, content-free audit, and transactional invalidation outbox records;
- public-HTTPS endpoint canonicalization, root-policy seams for exact private endpoints, dangerous-address denial, DNS revalidation, redirect/environment-proxy denial contracts, and TLS requirements;
- allowlisted Control Bot command routing and durable, expiring, cancellable, per-field non-secret configuration sessions; API-key-like Bot input is rejected before backend persistence;
- a key-only Web App with Telegram `initData` verification, five-minute admin/role/action/deployment/CAS-bound one-time launch proof, PostgreSQL rate limits, strict origin/body/schema checks, CSP/no-store headers, no key read/status API, empty success responses, and no third-party resource or browser storage use; and
- Windows static/type/unit/property/contract evidence using synthetic values only. Local Docker service tests are `NOT RUN`, and the matching Chromium binary is unavailable in the current restricted Windows environment, so browser evidence is `BLOCKED` locally rather than inferred from ASGI tests.

These M2 controls are component-level claims, not production enablement. GitLab Linux pipeline #2748486868 validated their migration, role, Chromium, and acceptance gates against signed commit `def4ff1f846307a7ea428de3c048616601cab7a4`; it did not validate a real provider, Telegram Bot/client, Caddy route, host key mount, production network policy, container, backup/restore, media, full orchestration, or live credential.

M3 additionally implements and tests:

- a framework-neutral Telegram port and Telethon 1.44.0 adapter that accepts only an injected client and peer resolver, preserving the design rule that the future `app` process is the sole Session owner;
- private non-Bot, non-self one-to-one admission; unsupported peers produce content-free event metadata with no conversation body, caption, file reference, media row, or model invocation;
- versioned update fingerprints, canonical message business keys, append-only revisions, delete-first tombstones, one-way redaction, stable album ordering, and content-free derived-memory reconciliation jobs;
- image download eligibility metadata without binary download, while voice, audio, video, video note, non-image document, and sticker data remain metadata-only;
- durable delivery groups, ordered outbound intents, payload hashes, stable non-zero 63-bit Telegram random IDs, one-way-completed attempts, explicit FloodWait/transient/permanent/unknown outcomes, and conservative crash recovery that never blindly returns an unknown send to pending;
- outgoing source reconciliation by stored random/message identity, explicit `system_pending` for ambiguous active sends, and human classification only when no system candidate exists;
- idempotent read high-watermarks, versioned typing leases, least-privilege M3 database grants, deterministic Telegram fakes, and content-free replay evidence; and
- Windows format, lint, strict typing, unit, and contract evidence using synthetic values only. Local disposable PostgreSQL tests are `NOT RUN` because no Docker daemon is available. GitLab Linux pipeline #2751916211 passed migration, role, repository, recovery, 12-case content-free replay, and M3-001 through M3-010 acceptance gates against disposable services and the signed evidence commit; it did not use a Telegram credential or validate live Telegram behavior.

These M3 controls are lifecycle primitives, not authorization to automate an account. The adapter could affect an account only if a separate caller supplies a live authenticated client, peer mapping, and explicit method call. The repository contains no Session, login/bootstrap flow, contact crawler, bulk-send loop, proactive scheduler, or default runtime wiring. Real Telegram behavior remains `NOT RUN`.

M4 additionally implements and tests:

- deterministic AUTO/HUMAN/COPILOT/PAUSED resolution with separately persisted operational BLOCKED state, resume floors, monotonic control/mode/content/generation versions, and fail-closed snapshot checks;
- immutable turn membership, sliding three-second debounce, ten-second hard cap, Main AI run/attempt identities, active-config/credential snapshots, usage fields, result hashes, and terminal late-result discard;
- an incoming-only three-second grace authorization that lists the exact allowed event delta and cannot authorize later edit, delete, human outgoing, control, mode, pause, maintenance, or policy changes;
- atomic normalized output, deterministic Telegram splitting, delivery group/intent creation, stable random IDs, and a repeated pre-RPC final gate with conversation/run/lease/source/idempotency checks;
- AUTO-only read acknowledgement and renewable typing/generation leases, with best-effort cancellation and typing stop when work becomes stale or terminal;
- durable human takeover invalidation, explicit `/reply_pending`, and reactive COPILOT draft/revision/action-token/approval state where every action token is admin/chat/purpose/revision/deadline bound and one-time; and
- framework-neutral Control Bot parsing that requires an opaque selected-contact token rather than a display name, plus content-free race and acceptance evidence writers.

These M4 controls are component-level claims. GitLab pipeline #2752242512 validated the prior baseline against disposable PostgreSQL 17.10/pgvector 0.8.6, Redis 8.2.8, deterministic fakes, 237 selected tests, four migration paths, and 14 content-free race cases; current continuation, queued-command, least-privilege, and JUnit-derived evidence repairs require a new pipeline before M4 can close again. The default CLI cannot reach them, the model port receives only a synthetic input fingerprint until M5 context assembly exists, and no live provider or Telegram composition root, polling loop, scheduler, container, or service unit is supplied. Real AUTO, real COPILOT delivery, and real Control Bot behavior remain `NOT RUN`.

The planned automated test suite uses deterministic synthetic fixtures, fake Telegram/provider services, disposable PostgreSQL/Redis containers, and content-free evidence by default. Production databases, real private conversations, personal Telegram accounts, and production Sessions are prohibited as test inputs. Real Telegram and Web App smoke tests are limited to an authorized dedicated staging account, Bot, and allowlisted test peers on an isolated host. Real provider smoke tests use fixed non-private text and test images with small limits. Ordinary and fork CI jobs receive no Telegram, provider, backup, or publication credentials; protected container, recovery, and soak work uses dedicated Ubuntu runners, while the real Telegram Session remains outside general CI. Routine sanitized test evidence is designed for 30-day retention and release, live, restore, and soak evidence for 365 days.

## Safeguards and controls beyond M4

Production completion still requires the following end-to-end controls. Some M2/M3 component primitives named here exist, but their Orchestrator/Bot/Caddy/container/host integration and live behavior are not implemented or validated:

- a single owner process for each Telethon Session;
- administrator allowlisting and Telegram Web App authentication;
- five-minute signed Web App authorization, one-time role/action-bound launch tokens, rate limits, no API-key read endpoint, and no third-party Web App analytics;
- account-default and contact-override AUTO, HUMAN, and COPILOT controls, with PAUSED, maintenance, and temporary HUMAN overlays;
- account-control, conversation-mode, content, turn, draft-revision, and approval checks that prevent stale AI results from being sent;
- no automatic backlog reply after pause, takeover, or dependency recovery unless the administrator explicitly invokes the pending-reply command;
- allowlisted, single-use, short-lived Control Bot action tokens for COPILOT approval, followed by an independent final send gate in the Telethon-owning `app` process;
- distinct `copilot_approved` provenance so approved AI drafts are not silently classified as independently written human messages;
- source-aware memory evidence rules that prevent AI or proactive output from independently proving contact facts or human writing style;
- application-side schema, scope, evidence, revision, conflict, and idempotency validation before a Memory Agent proposal can become active;
- default candidate review for low-confidence, ambiguous, or image-only memory proposals, with allowlisted preview and second confirmation for candidate acceptance or memory forget;
- metadata-only context inspection by default, with full context preview restricted to an allowlisted administrator, bound to an exact manifest and source-revision hash, gated by a single-use five-minute confirmation token, and revalidated against redaction immediately before reconstruction;
- in-memory-only context-preview reconstruction, no preview body in request, token, delivery, queue, log, or audit records, no image-binary access by the Control Bot, and tracked best-effort deletion of known Bot message IDs;
- immutable summary source membership and isolated embedding spaces so deleted evidence or vectors from different model spaces cannot remain silently active;
- deterministic proactive reason filtering with no model-discovered contacts or candidates, no proactive image input, and no missed-window backlog after downtime;
- default proactive account/contact daily budgets, relationship intervals, a 30-minute activity suppression window, atomic budget reservation, and final send gates in the Telethon-owning process;
- default `22:00-08:00` proactive quiet hours and an absolute `00:00-07:00` no-send window; only explicit `event_upcoming` or `promise_due` evidence with importance at least 0.90 and a latest useful time before 08:00 may use the `22:00-24:00` or `07:00-08:00` shoulder windows, at most once per contact local day;
- automatic proactive sending in AUTO mode only after all gates, proactive draft approval in COPILOT mode, and no proactive model run or backlog in HUMAN, PAUSED, maintenance, or blocked states;
- API key input through HTTPS rather than Telegram messages;
- application-layer credential encryption with the database ciphertext separated from the master key;
- AES-256-GCM credential encryption with deployment/role/version-bound authenticated data, versioned key rotation, and separately stored offline recovery material;
- independent credentials and immutable configuration payload versions for each logical model role;
- API key read responses, logs, errors, and audit records that never reveal the complete key;
- normalized message events that do not copy message bodies, one-way content redaction, and explicit data-erasure jobs;
- Caddy-only public TCP 443 exposure, private health/metrics, host firewalling, encrypted disks, non-root/read-only application containers, dropped capabilities, and no Docker Socket mount;
- exact model-endpoint URL canonicalization, redirect/proxy denial, DNS/IP revalidation, TLS verification, and a root-managed private-endpoint allowlist;
- encrypted off-host PostgreSQL/WAL and Session backups, independent erasure-ledger replay, manual restore approval, and no automatic database downgrade;
- fixed image byte/pixel/time limits, media and disk quotas, bounded retention, and fail-closed disk-pressure states;
- synthetic-only test fixtures, fake-by-default external services, protected runners, no rerun-to-PASS policy, secret/artifact scanning, and explicit `PASS`/`FAIL`/`NOT RUN`/`BLOCKED` evidence; and
- private PostgreSQL and Redis network exposure.

These are production and end-to-end requirements, not claims that the current implementation enables them by default. Future releases must test and accurately disclose which controls are implemented, wired, and enabled.

## Privileges and persistence

A future deployment will hold credentials capable of controlling a Telegram account and accessing configured model APIs and backup repositories. It will require persistent storage for the Telethon Session and application data, encrypted off-host backup storage, and outbound network access to Telegram, model providers, S3-compatible storage, ACME, and a configured alert target. Protected integration and recovery runners will require tightly scoped Docker access to disposable test containers; the dedicated live Telegram test host will hold a separate staging Session. Neither capability is exposed to the application, ordinary CI, forks, or public artifacts. The production design does not require arbitrary host command execution, container-management access, privilege escalation, or public database access.

## Residual risks

Even with the planned controls:

- compromise of the host, Telethon Session, Control Bot token, administrator Telegram account, master key, or model credential may expose sensitive data or account control;
- configured generation and embedding providers may receive highly sensitive private-message content and derived relationship or memory data, subject to those providers' own retention, logging, and jurisdictional behavior;
- encrypted off-host database and Session backups create additional high-value copies; compromise of the backup target or recovery material may expose account control or long-term private data;
- an incorrect mode, prompt, memory, timezone, event/intention projection, relationship inference, or proactive decision may send an unwanted, inaccurate, or inappropriately timed message;
- administrator changes to proactive limits or quiet-hour policy may increase unwanted-message risk even though the absolute no-send window, candidate allowlist, idempotency, evidence, activity, and final-gate requirements remain mandatory in the design;
- an incorrect extraction, summary, conflict decision, candidate approval, or embedding retrieval may preserve or surface inaccurate or overly sensitive information;
- an explicitly confirmed context preview copies highly sensitive material into Telegram; notifications, client caches, forwards, screenshots, and Telegram-controlled copies may persist after best-effort deletion;
- a Control Bot send that becomes `send_unknown` may have created a preview message without yielding a recoverable message ID, preventing reliable automatic deletion, and deletion requests for known messages may also fail;
- contacts may not distinguish AI-generated and human-generated messages under the shared identity;
- long-term storage increases privacy and breach impact;
- a browser, keyboard, Telegram client, notification system, JavaScript runtime, DNS resolver, reverse proxy, backup service, or alert destination may retain metadata or transient sensitive material outside the application's deletion guarantees;
- media expiration and the no-media-backup policy can make old images unavailable even when their message metadata remains;
- compromise or misconfiguration of a protected runner, staging Telegram account, live-provider job, synthetic-fixture review, or evidence upload could expose test credentials, contact external systems, or retain unintended sensitive data;
- backup, erasure, disk, resource, and recovery controls are currently unimplemented architecture requirements, and stated RPO/RTO values are not verified; and
- Telegram or model-provider policy changes may make a previously valid deployment non-compliant.

Operators are responsible for securing the host, obtaining appropriate authorization, setting conservative defaults, reviewing logs and proactive behavior, and stopping the service when account or credential compromise is suspected.

## Source identity and integrity

The public source of record is the GitLab repository above. No public package, image, tag, or binary is currently published. Source changes made by the automated coding agent are required to use a signed Git commit. Consumers should verify the commit identity and review the exact source revision they use.

## Reporting security or misuse concerns

Contact the repository maintainer through the GitLab account `Septuagintks`. Do not place Telegram Sessions, API keys, Bot tokens, private messages, or other sensitive evidence in a public issue. Ask the maintainer for a private reporting channel before sharing sensitive details.
